    "content": "Video: OPSEC: How To Stay Anonymous Online\nSource: https://www.youtube.com/channel/UCUvkPEm38w6pq8w5QYgtPPw/videos (YouTube)\nTranscript: --- OPSEC: How To Stay Anonymous Online ---\nSo let me paint you a picture. You open your \nlaptop, browse a few websites, maybe search for  \nsomething mildly interesting, close the tab, and \nthink nothing of it. Meanwhile your ISP has logged  \nevery DNS query you made, your browser fingerprint \nhas been recorded by six different ad networks,  \nyour real IP address is sitting in three \ndifferent server logs, and somewhere a  \ndata broker has just updated your profile with \ntwo new inferences about your political views  \nbased on what you read. You didn't do anything \nwrong. You didn't click anything suspicious.  \nYou just existed on the internet in the \ndefault configuration and the default  \nconfiguration is a surveillance machine that \nyou're living inside of without really noticing.\nOPSEC stands for Operational Security and it \ncomes from military intelligence the practice of  \nidentifying what information about yourself you're \nleaking and systematically cutting those leaks  \noff. In the context of staying anonymous online \nit's not one tool and it's not one setting. It's  \na layered approach where each layer covers a gap \nthe previous one left open. Tonight I'm going to  \nwalk you through exactly what I use and why, in \nthe order that actually makes sense to build it.\nThe first layer is DNS and most people \nnever think about it at all. Every time  \nyou type a website address your device sends \na DNS query to translate that name into an  \nIP address and by default that query goes to \nyour ISP's DNS server completely unencrypted.\nYour ISP can see every domain you \nlook up. Not the content of what  \nyou do on those sites that's handled \nby HTTPS but every single domain name,  \nwith timestamps. The fix is encrypted \nDNS and specifically DNS over HTTPS or  \nDNS over TLS. Switch your DNS resolver to one that \nsupports it Cloudflare's 1.1.1.1 with DoH enabled,  \nNextDNS if you want filtering on top of \nit, or better yet run your own Unbound  \nresolver locally. This is t",
